# PART 5 — DOCUMENT UPLOAD AND VALIDATION ENGINE

## 5.1 Dynamic checklist

For each selected application, display a table containing:

- Document ID.
- Document name.
- APH reference.
- Mandatory/conditional status.
- Applicability reason.
- Accepted file formats and configured size limit.
- Required issuing authority/signatory, where specified.
- Issue date, where relevant.
- Expiry date, where applicable.
- Submission status.
- Validation status.
- Uploaded filename.
- Upload timestamp.
- Version number.
- Reviewer remarks.
- Deficiency status.

Allow the applicant to upload only permitted file formats and validate file type and size on the server.

Use private storage and signed download URLs with appropriate expiry. Never expose public URLs for sensitive documents.

## 5.2 Document statuses

Use clearly defined states:
- NOT_UPLOADED
- UPLOADED
- AWAITING_VALIDATION
- VALIDATION_IN_PROGRESS
- VERIFIED
- DEFICIENCY_FOUND
- REPLACEMENT_REQUESTED
- REPLACEMENT_SUBMITTED
- REVALIDATION_PENDING
- ACCEPTED_FOR_WORKFLOW
- REQUIRES_HUMAN_REVIEW

Do not equate a file being uploaded with the document being verified or accepted.

## 5.3 Deterministic validation

Where the relevant data is available, check:
- Presence of required documents.
- Allowed file type and size.
- Required structured metadata.
- Applicable issue/expiry date rules.
- Required digital-signature status through a supported verification mechanism.
- Institution/application identifiers where reliably available.
- Differences between declared structured fields and stored records.
- Duplicate submissions.
- Conditional requirements.

Do not claim that scanned signatures are cryptographically verified merely because a signature image is visible.

If automated extraction of a scanned document is introduced, treat extracted values as untrusted until checked against deterministic validation and human review. No generative AI may determine regulatory compliance.

