# PART 8 — SCRUTINY AND DEFICIENCY NOTIFICATIONS

When an authorised scrutiny official records a deficiency, the institution must receive a notification.

Each deficiency record must include:
- Deficiency ID.
- Application ID.
- Application category.
- Stage where it was identified.
- Document or field concerned.
- Deficiency type.
- Detailed explanation.
- Exact APH reference, where applicable.
- Whether the issue is regulatory, administrative or awaiting human verification.
- Timestamp detected and timestamp communicated.
- Responsible reviewer role.
- Required corrective action.
- Deadline and deadline source.
- Current status.
- Resubmission history.

Supported deficiency types:
- Missing document.
- Expired/invalid document.
- Incomplete document.
- Signature/digital-signature issue.
- Institution or application mismatch.
- Inconsistent fields.
- Incorrect format.
- Conditional document not supplied.
- Human verification required.

The official must record a meaningful reason and, where applicable, the exact requirement that has not been met.

Do not invent a correction deadline. If the APH does not prescribe the deadline, it must be set by an authorised official under an approved operational policy and clearly labelled accordingly.

## 8.1 Deficiency resolution

The applicant must be able to:
1. Open the deficiency notice.
2. Understand what is wrong and why.
3. See the affected document or field.
4. See the due date and issuing authority.
5. Upload a corrected or missing document.
6. Add a response note where permitted.
7. Submit the response for re-scrutiny.
8. View the response timestamp and re-scrutiny status.

Preserve all previous document versions.

## 8.2 Re-scrutiny

When a correction is submitted:
- Create a new document version.
- Link it to the deficiency it addresses.
- Route the case to the authorised re-scrutiny queue.
- Revalidate only the changed documents and dependent rules, plus any other checks explicitly required by the applicable APH procedure.
- Preserve the original scrutiny findings.
- Record every new finding.

If no further deficiency is recorded and the authorised official completes the relevant checks, route the application to the next applicable APH stage.

Do not automatically forward an application merely because the uploaded files pass a technical validation.

