# PART 19 — IMPLEMENTATION ORDER

Build incrementally in the following sequence:

Phase 1: Institutional dashboard shell, navigation and profile form.

Phase 2: Searchable 22-category application selector and application-specific form structure.

Phase 3: Verified APH document requirements engine and secure document centre.

Phase 4: Application submission, unique application ID, receipt and persistent timeline.

Phase 5: Deficiency notifications, replacement documents and re-scrutiny workflow.

Phase 6: EVC visit schedule, committee information and applicant notifications.

Phase 7: Post-EVC review status and authorised decision communications.

Phase 8: Appeal eligibility, seven-day deadline tracking and verified committee routing.

Phase 9: Compliance monitoring, reminders and audit history.

Phase 10: End-to-end security testing and usability refinement.

Start with Phase 1 and establish a working application structure. Continue with the remaining phases using maintainable components, migrations and tests. Do not attempt to implement all phases in one giant unverified code generation.

At the end of each phase, report:
- Files created or modified.
- Features actually implemented.
- Database changes.
- Configuration required.
- Tests run and their actual results.
- Unresolved APH questions.
- Remaining tasks.

FINAL GOAL:
Deliver a functional, secure, traceable Institutional Dashboard that allows an authorised institution representative to file and track applications, resolve deficiencies, receive EVC and decision updates, submit eligible appeals and monitor compliance through one unified portal.

The dashboard must automate administrative coordination and verified deterministic checks without replacing human statutory authority or claiming unauthorised integration with AICTE systems.